{"kind":"propose","text":"CURRENT-WORLD RE-ANCHOR OF THE ADOPTED SUPPORTED-HOST DECISION. The full decision below is semantically identical to proposal 8a758fb795b459f78832efa3ca9aee02c226a6d2. It is restated only because the approved UI-only merge replaced the broad internal/service artifact; that merge changed generated bundle bytes, not the resident trust boundary. Independent design approval 05c1062c962ec838bf43fd593b42fb4f05bb37f0, Codex review ratification f854248867b540c5fa82026f9540b4d72b14250d, and Hugh adoption bc758b7a614ce25041485a3544321afa50836bbb remain verified history and are named here rather than used as stale structural bases.\n\nREVISED SUPPORTED-HOST DECISION. This replaces proposal 7b507ca7 and incorporates the later changes-requested evidence in 105cefd without using that stale review as authority.\n\nDecision: support the signing resident only where every process that can reach it or read its repository key storage is trusted as the same operator. Do not claim shared-host or mutually untrusted local-process support.\n\nObserved boundary. The resident listens on TCP loopback, which limits network location but does not identify a client. Current MCP clients choose an actor and a private session identifier when announcing presence. The resident binds that identifier once, then /v0/say and /v0/act use it as a bearer credential and the application signs with the selected actor key. A client can renew the identifier indefinitely; the lease expires only when renewals stop, and departure or resident restart ends it. Public handles are unrelated to the bearer value, but current hygiene is incomplete: the departure URL contains the private identifier, MCP whoami exposes it to tool transcripts, and HTTP errors may include request URLs. Any local process able to reach the port can announce any actor whose key files internal/app can open and obtain a genuinely actor-signed act. The fold may later judge the act ineffective, but signatures cannot recover intent.\n\nChosen contract. A future implementation must make the resident trust boundary explicit and fail closed unless its operator deliberately acknowledges trusted local resident clients. This acknowledgement discloses only the resident boundary; it cannot make the host safe, protect direct gs CLI key access, or turn same-account malware into a trusted client. Non-loopback listeners remain refused. The listener must resolve localhost and require every resolved address to be loopback, rather than trusting a hostname spelling. Mutating HTTP endpoints must reject hostile web-page and cross-origin use before side effects through an explicit Origin/Host/content-type policy with no permissive CORS path.\n\nCredential correction. The resident, not the client, must mint the private session credential after accepting the actor selection inside the acknowledged trust boundary. It must use system randomness with a fixed minimum entropy and validated opaque shape, bind exactly one repository and actor, never appear in presence, ordinary status, logs, diagnostics, durable events, referrers, query strings, path segments, tool transcripts or returned URL errors, and become invalid on departure, non-renewed lease expiry, resident restart or explicit revocation. The existing public unrelated handle remains display-only and never authorizes an act. This credential hardening limits accidental disclosure and replay; it is not authentication of an untrusted local process.\n\nStorage and signing. Repository actor keys are application-host custody managed through internal/app and local key files above the kernel. The supported boundary includes the OS account, repository common directory, backups, snapshots, developer tooling and sync services that can copy those files. Keys and any future capabilities must not enter unprotected backups or shared sync; protected or encrypted copies expand the documented recovery boundary. The sequencing kernel and fold contracts do not change: a signature proves key possession, while the fold separately decides semantic force.\n\nAuthenticated-client alternative, deferred. Shared-host support requires identity before actor selection and before any key use. TCP loopback has no portable peer identity. A platform-native local transport could bind an OS principal only with a governed principal-to-actor policy and fail-closed unsupported-platform behavior. A capability works only if it is unavailable to unauthorized processes, repository- and actor-scoped, unforgeable, revocable and rotatable; a file readable by the same account does not stop same-account malware. A resident holding every actor key remains a wildcard custodian. This is a separate architecture and custody feature, not a flag on the present HTTP service.\n\nCompromise and recovery. A leaked live session compromises its bound actor until revocation, departure, lapse without renewal or restart. An untrusted process able to announce sessions or read local key files compromises every accessible actor. Stop the resident, preserve the append-only log, invalidate sessions, secure the host and all copies, audit the exposure window, record disputes durably, retire compromised principals and custody under normal authority, create replacements, and rotate any exposed sequencer or future capability material. Never delete or rewrite signed history.\n\nDeployment. Supported examples are a dedicated trusted workstation account or an equivalently isolated VM/container namespace with no untrusted peer process and no exposed key-copy path. Generic loopback, generic containerization and file mode alone are not evidence of this boundary. Startup output, resident status, browser guidance and MCP guidance must say plainly that every process inside the acknowledged resident boundary can act as every actor key the application can open.\n\nA future shared-host claim must prove: unauthorized principals cannot create, renew, use or end another actor session and cannot append a commit or frame; identity is bound before actor choice or key access; credentials cannot cross actors or repositories or be replayed after expiry/revocation; all mutating endpoints and browser-origin paths reject before side effects; secret material is scoped, high-entropy, non-disclosing, rotatable and outside unauthorized storage and backups; restart/revocation works without rewriting history; unsupported platforms and TCP fallback refuse shared-host mode; adversarial separate-process and separate-principal tests cover guessing, theft, replay, hostile origins, cross-actor and cross-repository confusion, renewal, departure and diagnostics, and state honestly whether malicious same-account processes remain out of scope.\n\nArchitecture: the decision sets the application-host and outer-adapter contract; nexus session binding and service/MCP transport sit inside it, while kernel verification and fold semantics remain unchanged. Security: it removes implied local authentication, corrects current credential leakage assumptions, and requires explicit fail-closed boundaries. Simplification: it chooses one honest supported mode now and leaves authenticated local transport to a separate ratified design instead of installing a partial authentication framework."}